1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,546
The map was finished, and the only question it had left behind was whether anyone had learned anything from it.

2
00:00:06,546 --> 00:00:11,295
The second arc opened with a signal, not with a plan.

3
00:00:11,295 --> 00:00:22,077
A researcher from the education faculty asked the group a single question at the end of a lecture: how would they prove that the map taught anyone anything?

4
00:00:22,077 --> 00:00:27,390
The early answer was a sketch; the later answer was a blueprint.

5
00:00:27,390 --> 00:00:36,108
“At every turn,” she said, “you will be asked for evidence, and such a demand is healthy.”

6
00:00:36,108 --> 00:00:38,828
The battle was against habit.

7
00:00:38,828 --> 00:00:49,165
A teacher, a columnist, and a novelist told the same story in different words: students memorize for a quiz and forget by the weekend.

8
00:00:49,165 --> 00:00:53,749
“Knowledge is not a diploma,” the instructor said.

9
00:00:53,749 --> 00:01:01,407
“A diploma is a receipt.” Several people in the room nodded; few wrote it down.

10
00:01:01,407 --> 00:01:09,395
A kid of nineteen in the second row asked whether a fresh example might help, and got one.

11
00:01:09,395 --> 00:01:23,501
The team interviewed a vocational trainer, a translator, and a biographer, and each described a different enemy: the commercial summary, the daily rush, and the timely excuse.

12
00:01:23,501 --> 00:01:28,235
“Every method has a mythology,” the historian said.

13
00:01:28,235 --> 00:01:42,946
“Ours is that reading is the same as learning.” A fishing village on the coast had produced a specialist in reading who could not name a single classic, and an educator who could name a thousand.

14
00:01:42,946 --> 00:01:51,979
Maya, a journalist who was now a columnist for the student paper, published a narrative about a dropout who returned.

15
00:01:51,979 --> 00:01:57,757
A reviewer called it classic; an essayist called it sentimental.

16
00:01:57,757 --> 00:02:16,438
The widow who ran the reading room said it was the first honest page she had read that month, and a sports commentator mailed her an old typewriter with a note: “Leisure reading saved my graduation.” They did not hesitate to print both reactions.

17
00:02:16,438 --> 00:02:23,136
An amateur planner in the curriculum office asked them to prevent the same mistake twice.

18
00:02:23,136 --> 00:02:28,050
Her teaching had one rule: a mystery is not a gap.

19
00:02:28,050 --> 00:02:41,331
“You may hotdog about your method,” she said, “but the method is what survives you.” But the eye of the faculty was on them now, and every page had to earn its place.

20
00:02:41,331 --> 00:02:51,374
Most of the volunteers agreed; many of them, in fact, had said the same thing a year ago, and no one had listened.

21
00:02:51,374 --> 00:03:01,561
By the end of the evening, quite a few of them could sit still with an unanswered question, which was at least a million times better than guessing.

22
00:03:01,561 --> 00:03:11,358
“Yeah,” Maya said, “that is the point.” Some of the older notes looked thin by comparison.
